---
/**
 * /work/ai-adoption — the case study, rebuilt from the PDF.
 *
 * The strongest single piece of evidence on the site, given a proper reading
 * layout. The two charts are inline SVG, drawn here from the numbers, not
 * screenshots of the PDF. Small and plain on purpose.
 *
 * Retention is the cohort as the case study states it: 6 enrolled, 4 active,
 * 3 completed. The frictions chart carries no magnitudes, because the study
 * reports none — five barriers, with trust marked as "the strongest signal in
 * the room", which is the only ranking the text makes.
 */
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/Prose.astro';

const cohort = [
	{ label: 'Enrolled', n: 6 },
	{ label: 'Active', n: 4 },
	{ label: 'Completed', n: 3 },
];

const frictions = [
	'No mental model',
	'Setup friction',
	'Cognitive load',
	'Trust assessment',
	'Identity resistance',
];
const strongest = 'Trust assessment';

const unit = 56; // px per participant
const left = 96; // label gutter
---

<PageLayout
	title="From AI quick wins to AI enabler"
	docTitle="AI Adoption Case Study"
	eyebrow="AI Adoption · Case study"
	description="Designing for adoption in a trust-cautious market."
	trail={[
		{ name: 'Work', path: '/work' },
		{ name: 'AI Adoption', path: '/work/ai-adoption' },
	]}
	headline
>
	<section class="section section--flush">
		<div class="shell">
			<Prose class="prose--plain prose--split">
				<h2>Berlin, 2026</h2>
				<p>
					Berlin, 2026. Four sessions, thirteen hours, six enrolled, three finished. A small cohort,
					and a useful one, because the people in it were exactly the professionals every AI product
					is currently trying to reach and mostly failing to keep.
				</p>
				<p>
					The goal was never tool exposure. It was adoption: getting AI into real working practice,
					against real privacy concerns, real tiredness and real scepticism about the companies
					involved.
				</p>

				<h2>What actually got in the way</h2>
				<p>
					Retention tracked normal adult evening-course patterns. The interesting part was why people
					stalled, and it was rarely technical ability.
				</p>

				<figure class="chart">
					<svg
						viewBox={`0 0 ${left + unit * 6 + 40} 132`}
						role="img"
						aria-labelledby="retention-title retention-desc"
					>
						<title id="retention-title">Cohort retention</title>
						<desc id="retention-desc">
							Six participants enrolled, four stayed active, three completed the course.
						</desc>
						{
							cohort.map((row, i) => (
								<g transform={`translate(0 ${i * 40 + 8})`}>
									<text class="chart__label" x="0" y="20">
										{row.label}
									</text>
									<rect
										class:list={['chart__bar', i === 2 && 'chart__bar--end']}
										x={left}
										y="4"
										width={row.n * unit}
										height="24"
									/>
									<text class="chart__value" x={left + row.n * unit + 10} y="21">
										{row.n}
									</text>
								</g>
							))
						}
					</svg>
					<figcaption>Cohort retention: enrolled, active, completed.</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<p>Five barriers came up consistently.</p>

				<p>
					<strong>No mental model.</strong> People had used the tools and still couldn't say what they
					were for. Without a model of where AI fits, every session starts from zero.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Setup friction.</strong> Accounts, versions, permissions, which tool does which thing.
					Small obstacles, but they arrive before any value does.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Cognitive load.</strong> This is an evening course. People come after a full working
					day. Anything requiring fresh concentration loses.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Trust assessment.</strong> The strongest signal in the room. Participants were actively
					evaluating what happens to their data, and they wanted an answer before they would engage, not
					after.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Identity resistance.</strong> Experienced professionals whose skill is the thing being
					automated. Not fear of the technology. Discomfort about what it means for what they're worth.
				</p>

				<figure class="chart">
					<svg viewBox="0 0 400 190" role="img" aria-labelledby="friction-title friction-desc">
						<title id="friction-title">The five adoption frictions</title>
						<desc id="friction-desc">
							No mental model, setup friction, cognitive load, trust assessment and identity
							resistance. Trust assessment was the strongest signal in the room.
						</desc>
						{
							frictions.map((label, i) => (
								<g transform={`translate(0 ${i * 36 + 8})`}>
									<circle
										class:list={['chart__dot', label === strongest && 'chart__dot--strong']}
										cx="12"
										cy="14"
										r="6"
									/>
									<text class="chart__label" x="32" y="19">
										{label}
									</text>
									{label === strongest && (
										<text class="chart__note" x="200" y="19">
											the strongest signal
										</text>
									)}
								</g>
							))
						}
					</svg>
					<figcaption>
						The five adoption frictions. Unranked, except the one the room made loudest.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<h2>What I changed</h2>
				<p>
					<strong>Frameworks over features.</strong> RCCO and TRAP, taught as transferable structures
					rather than tool tutorials.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Workflows over prompts.</strong> Every demonstration was an end-to-end piece of real
					work, not an isolated clever instruction.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>A personal win in every session.</strong> Everyone left each session with one output
					from their own job that they could use the next morning.
				</p>

				<h2>What shifted</h2>
				<p>
					Participants arrived seeing AI as either a novelty or a threat. By session three, they were
					structuring their own prompts, chaining outputs, and rejecting tools on their own judgment.
				</p>
				<p>
					The real indicator of adoption wasn't fluency. It was agency. People stopped asking what AI
					could do and started deciding what it was allowed to do in their work.
				</p>

				<h2>What this means if you build or sell AI products</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>Trust is a conversion mechanism in European markets, not a compliance footnote.</li>
					<li>People adopt a use case before they adopt a tool.</li>
					<li>Clarity beats feature breadth. Every time.</li>
					<li>The first successful personal output predicts whether someone comes back.</li>
					<li>Professional identity is a hidden variable in every adoption curve.</li>
					<li>Tool proliferation has created a selection problem, and selection is a trust problem.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					Adoption is rarely blocked by intelligence or curiosity. It's blocked by uncertainty: about
					privacy, about usefulness, about fit, about what it says about you that you're using it.
					Products that reduce that uncertainty, show relevance immediately, and frame themselves as
					augmentation get adopted. The rest get tried once.
				</p>
			</Prose>
		</div>
	</section>
</PageLayout>

<style>
	.chart {
		margin-block: 2em;
		max-width: 28rem;
	}

	.chart svg {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		overflow: visible;
	}

	.chart figcaption {
		margin-block-start: var(--sp-2);
		font-family: var(--font-ui);
		font-size: var(--fs-small);
		color: var(--ink-muted);
	}

	/* SVG text does not inherit the prose font; set it so the chart reads in the
	   site's own voices. */
	.chart__label,
	.chart__value,
	.chart__note {
		font-family: var(--font-ui);
		font-size: 13px;
		fill: var(--ink-soft);
	}

	.chart__value {
		font-weight: 600;
		fill: var(--ink);
	}

	.chart__note {
		font-style: italic;
		fill: var(--ember);
	}

	.chart__bar {
		fill: var(--ink-muted);
	}

	.chart__bar--end {
		fill: var(--ember);
	}

	.chart__dot {
		fill: none;
		stroke: var(--ink-muted);
		stroke-width: 1.5;
	}

	.chart__dot--strong {
		fill: var(--ember);
		stroke: var(--ember);
	}
</style>
